{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0141e385c8acf45b0169bb41c37afc819197020a55d51c5924", "created": 1773879747, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Cancer Recurrence: Risk Assessment, Surveillance, and Treatment Pathways\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community centers on the clinical concept of cancer recurrence and the interconnected processes used to anticipate, detect, and manage it. The core node is RECURRENCE, which links to treatment decision frameworks (risk-stratified guideline content), diagnostic workup for suspected return (imaging and lab evaluation), and escalation strategies (surgery and repeat therapy lines). Around recurrence, RISK ASSESSMENT classifies patients/tumors into risk categories (including “recurrent tumor” as inherently high-risk in some skin cancer frameworks), while surveillance concepts describe structured follow-up, the “no evidence of disease” state, and imaging schedules designed to detect recurrence early. Treatment capabilities described within the network emphasize chemotherapy as a central modality for recurrence and biomarker testing (e.g., MMR, FRα expression, RET mutations) to guide therapy choices in recurrent disease contexts.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"RECURRENCE is the central organizing concept, defined by return after a cancer-free period and spanning multiple cancer types and management contexts\",\n      \"explanation\": \"RECURRENCE is explicitly defined as cancer returning after treatment and after a period with no detectable disease, with the cancer-free interval itself represented as a related concept. This definition anchors downstream decisions about when to initiate additional evaluation, monitoring, and treatment. [Data: Entities (21, 6189); Relationships (14163)]\\n\\nThe recurrence concept in this network is not limited to a single tumor type: it